  "notes": "Multi-instrumentalist pop producer of boyband and post-disco pop. The documented method is loop-first and it is the whole drum block: \"the main guitar riff and drums on his biggest recent single are Splice sample-pack loops, over which he plays live bass, drums, guitar, keys and percussion himself; so expect a sampled groove skeleton with played humanising layers, tambourine on offbeats, and no trap hat vocabulary.\" That is a high `quantizeStrength` on the kick and snare with real jitter on the played percussion, `tambourine` at `offbeat` placement, and `rolls.freqPerBar` 0 — the absence of the roll vocabulary against a parent that runs rolls at 0.2 is a real delta. No `bass808` block: \"no 808. He plays syncopated 16th-note funk/disco electric bass, mixed loud as a second hook\", which is `independent_riff` at 0.32 with a full passing-tone set and a high `anticipationProb`. Harmony is \"2- or 4-chord vamps with 7ths\" with \"diatonic majors with dominant-7th and major-6th funk colour on the disco records, and IV—V—I anthem cadences on the band records\", so both family types are present and `harmonicRhythm` is vamp-led. The arrangement carries what the band half of his catalogue actually is: \"he arranges by redistributing solo lines across voices section by section, with John Ryan as co-writer/co-producer\" — John Ryan has no separate entry in the research and is folded in here rather than duplicated — which is the `call_response` structure with a 2-bar phrase, plus \"the title hook inside the first four bars of the chorus\" and the documented \"drums-out or bass-only bar before the last chorus\". ⚠ `confidence` is medium against a high-medium rating because every drum probability, the humanize numbers, the harmonic weights, the melody register and `bpm.mode` 115 (the midpoint of the stated 100—130 band) are INFERRED.",
